# Build 414: lesson top-ups for the 7 replacement books (3 each, to 8).

EXTRA = {
"seven-principles-marriage": [
{"title": "Accept Influence or Manufacture a Power Struggle", "chapter": "Principle 4", "summary": "John Gottman's data on this point is stark: marriages where the husband refuses to share power and accept influence from his wife fail at dramatically higher rates, because every decision becomes a referendum on whose reality rules. Accepting influence is not surrender; it is treating your partner's concerns as data about a shared life. His exercises ask couples to practise yielding on small things visibly, building the muscle before the large decisions, money, moves, in laws, arrive with their heavier weather.", "example": "In the lab, a husband who said let us ask what you think would work, and meant it, could disagree on everything else and still score as a master. The sentence functioned as a pressure valve for the whole system, because his wife's nervous system had evidence that her voice changed outcomes in that house.", "action": "Yield visibly on one small decision this week, and say why: your view makes sense to me. Watch what it buys in the next big one."},
{"title": "Shared Meaning Is Built, Not Found", "chapter": "Principle 7", "summary": "The final principle is the marriage's culture: the rituals of connection, shared goals, roles and symbols that make two biographies into a household with its own story. John Gottman found masters narrate their struggles as a joint epic, us against the problem, with humour about the early hardships. Couples build this deliberately: how we do mornings, what this family celebrates, what the money is ultimately for. Without shared meaning, the first six principles are techniques in search of a country to defend.", "example": "Couples who could tell their story with a strong we, including the bad years reframed as chapters survived together, scored highest on every measure in the lab. Their secret was not a better past. It was editorial: the same events, narrated as a campaign they had served in together rather than a trial of each other.", "action": "Found one ritual of connection this week: a farewell, a reunion, a Friday review. Name what your household stands for in one sentence together."},
{"title": "The Repair Attempt Is the Real Marital Skill", "chapter": "Throughout the Research", "summary": "John Gottman's most hopeful finding: the success of a repair attempt, the joke mid argument, the touch, the let me try that again, predicts a marriage's future better than the absence of fighting. Failed repairs, not fights, are the danger signal, because they mean the friendship system can no longer interrupt the conflict system. Couples are taught to make repairs clumsily and to receive them generously, since a rejected repair, however graceless, doubles the cost of the next attempt.", "example": "Masters in conflict studies deployed repairs constantly, a silly face at minute four of an argument that would have escalated for an hour in other couples, and their partners, crucially, let the repairs work. The divorced couples made the same attempts and watched them get swatted away, after which the argument owned the evening and eventually the archive.", "action": "Make one repair attempt in your next disagreement, however clumsy, and if your partner makes one, accept it visibly before returning to the point."}
],
"into-thin-air": [
{"title": "Summit Fever Is a Team Disease", "chapter": "The Analysis", "summary": "Jon Krakauer anatomises summit fever as social, not just personal: when clients have paid fortunes, sponsors expect photographs and guides compete on summit rates, turning back becomes a group betrayal rather than a judgement. Individuals who might privately choose descent find the decision made by the rope. His chapters on the other expeditions sharing the mountain that day show the fever crossing teams, each rope of climbers taking the next rope's continued ascent as evidence that conditions must be acceptable, a cascade of borrowed courage.", "example": "Climbers later described watching others continue upward and revising their own fear downward, though the weather had not changed. The mountain that day hosted a market in reassurance, each participant both buyer and seller, and the currency collapsed all at once in the storm.", "action": "In your team, name who is authorised, indeed celebrated, for calling the descent. If nobody is, appoint them before the summit push, not during it."},
{"title": "The Clients Who Lived Questioned the Product", "chapter": "Aftermath", "summary": "The survivors' reckonings differ, but several converge on Jon Krakauer's hardest question: what exactly had they purchased? The honest answer, a chance, logistics, company, could not be sold at those prices without the implied answer, the summit. The book's commercial analysis predicted the industry's reforms and its evasions alike: better screening, more oxygen, higher prices, and the same fundamental sale. Readers in every guided industry, from finance to education, recognise the brochure and the fine print, which is why the book escaped the mountaineering shelf within a year.", "example": "One client who survived with severe frostbite spent his recovery defending the expedition he had paid to join, while another became a critic of commercial Everest. Jon Krakauer prints both without adjudicating their grief, letting the reader see how the product shapes even the testimony about it.", "action": "Read the fine print of your most ambitious purchase this year: what is actually promised, and what did the brochure let you assume? Price the gap."},
{"title": "Jon Krakauer's Own Account Settles Nothing, Which Is the Point", "chapter": "The Writing of the Book", "summary": "Jon Krakauer reached the summit, descended early, and later learned that a climber he reported dead had been misidentified in the chaos, among other errors of that night that his reporting initially carried. The book's later editions wrestle publicly with disputes, notably with guide Anatoly Boukreev's defenders, over who failed whom above the South Col. Critics assign the quarrel itself a lesson: even an honest witness at altitude produces a partial record, and the families of the dead must live among competing versions. The memoir's authority comes from admitting this inside its own pages.", "example": "He revised the account across editions and printings, appending responses and corrections, an almost unheard of practice for a bestseller. Mountaineering libraries shelve the rival books facing each other; thoughtful readers are expected to climb both.", "action": "Where your version of a disaster is the record, append your corrections visibly. It is the only known antidote to becoming the official story."}
],
"essential-rumi": [
{"title": "Grief Is the Price and the Doorway", "chapter": "On Grief", "summary": "Rumi did not transcend his grief for Shams; he metabolised it into forty thousand verses, and the poems keep instructing mourners that sorrow is not the opposite of the spiritual life but its furnace. The Essential Rumi returns to the theme in every register: grief as a clearing wind, as the wound where light enters, as the price of having loved anything real. Readers who arrive via bereavement find the poems conspicuously unsentimental: the pain is not explained, it is accompanied, which after eight centuries remains the rarer service.", "example": "He wrote that he was a sober man of learning who became, through love and loss, a man who whirls. The dervish dance itself, born in the legend from his turning at a goldsmith's hammering, is grief converted to motion, still practised by the order he founded, still recognisable to anyone who has paced a house at night converting sorrow into steps.", "action": "Convert one grief into motion this week: walk it, dance it, work it with your hands, and notice what the body files that the mind could not."},
{"title": "Do Not Be Satisfied With Stories", "chapter": "On Direct Experience", "summary": "A famous line warns against settling for how the world ends in other people's telling: Rumi wants the reader thirsty at the actual water. His poems repeatedly demote doctrine, reputation and second hand spirituality in favour of direct encounter, the taste over the description of sweetness. Coleman Barks arranges several of these as challenges to the religious professional Rumi himself had been, making the collection useful to readers allergic to organised piety who still suspect the mystics knew something about their own minds.", "example": "He mocks the man who polishes the mirror of his reputation while his heart rusts, in images that have survived translation into advertising slogans and tattoo ink alike. The scholarly Rumi would perhaps wince; the poet who signed away his own name to his friend would recognise the theft as the compliment it is.", "action": "Take one belief you hold second hand, about God, yourself, or another person, and test it against one direct experience this week."},
{"title": "The Reed Flute's Lament Is the Human Condition", "chapter": "The Opening Image", "summary": "The Masnavi, Rumi's six book epic, opens with a reed flute cut from its reed bed, whose music is the sound of separation longing for return. Coleman Barks includes versions of the image because it organises everything after: we are cut things, making music out of the cut. The Essential Rumi is best read with that flute audible: every poem about love, grief or God is a variation on homesickness and the strange fact that the longing itself has become beautiful, which is why the sad poems so often sound like celebrations.", "example": "Whirling dervishes still turn to reed flute music in Konya, where Rumi's tomb draws more visitors than almost any site in Turkey, Muslim pilgrims and secular poetry tourists queuing together. The flute's complaint turns out to be an accurate prediction of its own audience: everyone arrives cut from somewhere.", "action": "Name your reed bed: the wholeness, place or person you were cut from. Make one piece of music, literal or otherwise, out of the cut this month."}
],
"yes-to-life": [
{"title": "Meaning Is Detected, Not Manufactured", "chapter": "Logotherapy's Core", "summary": "Viktor E. Frankl insists meaning is not invented by the self or assigned by a guru; it is detected in the world's specific claims: the work waiting for your hands, the person waiting for your love, the suffering waiting for your stance. The lectures give the method a name, conscience as the organ of meaning, tuned by attention the way an ear is tuned by listening. This is why his therapy sends people outward rather than inward: the self that goes looking for itself directly finds mostly mirrors, while the self forgotten in a task finds, incidentally, itself.", "example": "His clinical stories from those years return to patients cured of despair not by insight into their childhoods but by one forward question answered concretely: who is waiting for what from you? The lectures show the question being asked of an entire city, which is why they still read like a public health intervention rather than a memoir.", "action": "Ask the outward question this morning: who or what is waiting for something only I can give today? Do that before checking anything else."},
{"title": "The Defiant Power of the Human Spirit", "chapter": "On Resistance", "summary": "The lectures name a capacity Viktor E. Frankl saw in the camps and refused to let the audience forget in peacetime: the spirit's power to take a stance against its own conditions, psychological, biological, social. He calls it defiant deliberately; this is not adjustment or acceptance but a kind of inner mutiny by which a person remains more than their circumstances' sum. In 1946 Vienna the doctrine had a political edge his listeners could not miss: a civilisation that had surrendered to conditions once must practise the mutiny daily or be ready to surrender again.", "example": "He describes deciding, on a forced march, to lecture in his mind about the psychology of the camps after liberation, converting the march itself into research material. The defiance is modest and total: the Nazis owned his body and schedule, and he spent both taking notes for a future they intended him never to have. He had the future. The notes became these lectures.", "action": "Practise one small mutiny today: convert a condition imposed on you into material, notes, training, evidence, for a future it did not intend."},
{"title": "To Suffer Well Is Not to Suffer Gladly", "chapter": "On the Stance", "summary": "Viktor E. Frankl closes the suffering lecture by separating dignity from masochism with surgical care: meaning through suffering is available only when suffering cannot be removed, and choosing it where removal is possible is sickness wearing virtue's clothes. The stance he praises is specific: bearing the unavoidable in such a way that it becomes testimony, proof to oneself and others that a human being is not reducible to their worst circumstances. The audience of 1946, sitting among ruins their own nation had authored and suffered, needed the distinction more than any slogan, and the lectures do not flinch from either direction.", "example": "He spoke of a young patient dying in his clinic who used her last weeks to console the staff around her bed, reversing the ward's emotional economy. Whether or not the anecdote can be verified, its function in the hall is clear: his listeners, many facing early deaths of every kind that year, were being shown a way to be patients without being only patients.", "action": "Write the testimony version of your hardest unavoidable thing: what would carrying it well prove, and to whom? Carry it that way for one week."}
],
"atlas-of-the-heart": [
{"title": "Stress and Overwhelm Are Different Countries", "chapter": "Places We Go When Things Are Uncertain", "summary": "One of the atlas's most used distinctions: stress is having too much to handle with resources that might stretch; overwhelm is the state where nothing can be processed at all, whose only correct response is doing nothing, rest as an intervention rather than a reward. Brené Brown found high achievers systematically mislabel overwhelm as stress and prescribe themselves effort, the one medicine guaranteed to deepen it. The chapter has been adopted by everyone from head teachers to special forces medics because it converts a shame state, I should be coping, into a triage category with a protocol.", "example": "She describes her own overwhelm protocol, learned the hard way: permission to be in no productivity at all for a defined stretch, walks without input, because the nervous system files overwhelm under threat. Readers report the entry alone justifying the book to their employers, cited, occasionally, in resignation letters and in the emails that prevented them.", "action": "Refile today's state honestly: stressed or overwhelmed? If overwhelmed, prescribe rest first, in writing, like medicine with a dose."},
{"title": "Comparison's Trio: Envy, Jealousy and Schadenfreude", "chapter": "Places We Go When We Compare", "summary": "The atlas disentangles the comparison family with unusual care: envy wants what another has and feels hostile scarcity; jealousy fears losing what we have to a rival; schadenfreude takes pleasure in another's fall and quietly predicts our own loneliness. Each, Brené Brown argues, is information about our insecurities wearing someone else's face. The practical yield is at the family table and the feed alike: naming which of the three is present converts a character indictment into a weather report the person can actually use.", "example": "A mother who believed she envied another mother's career discovered, in the atlas's definitions, that she feared her own children's preference, jealousy, and was able to ask for reassurance instead of starting a competition she did not want. The right word ended an arms race neither party had declared.", "action": "Catch one comparison feeling this week and file it precisely: envy, jealousy or schadenfreude. Ask what insecurity it is reporting."},
{"title": "Belonging Versus Fitting In: The Book's Moral Centre", "chapter": "Places We Go in Search of Connection", "summary": "Brené Brown's research distinction, beloved of every school that adopts her: fitting in is contorting yourself to be accepted, belonging is being accepted as you are, and the first is actually the barrier to the second. The atlas entry audits the places we perform this confusion, workplaces, families, feeds, and gives her most quoted practical counsel, do not walk through the world looking for evidence that you do not belong, because you will always find it. The sentence has been printed on more staffroom walls than any in the book, for actuarial reasons.", "example": "Students in her research described betraying themselves in small denominations, laughing at jokes that wounded their own kind, editing accents and opinions, and reported the same dividend: the belonging purchased never arrived, while the self who might have received it was out of the room. Teachers call the entry the whole anti bullying curriculum in four pages.", "action": "Notice one fitting in contortion this week and decline it once, gently. Log what the belonging account does with the honesty."}
],
"difficult-conversations": [
{"title": "Feelings Are the Main Event Pretending to Be a Side Issue", "chapter": "The Feelings Conversation", "summary": "Unexpressed feelings, the authors show, leak: into tone, sarcasm, absenteeism and the sudden explosion at the third minor incident. Their claim is stronger than permission to vent. Feelings are the core subject of most difficult conversations, and negotiating them well, describing rather than acting out, listening for the other's with the same seriousness, is what resolution is made of. They also teach the audit that precedes expression: feelings are generated by our interpretations, so the same event can honestly produce different weather in different people, and both weathers are data.", "example": "A salary conversation that keeps failing on arithmetic resolves in their case study when the employee's actual feeling, feeling unseen by a mentor she admired, gets the floor the numbers were substituting for. The raise, when it came, was smaller than demanded and entirely sufficient, because the conversation had finally been about its subject.", "action": "Before your next hard talk, list your feelings in full sentences, then the interpretations generating them. Bring the first; hold the second as hypothesis."},
{"title": "Start From the Third Story", "chapter": "Beginning the Conversation", "summary": "Every dispute has your story, their story, and the third story: the neutral account a documentary would give of how this situation came to be, which both parties can usually accept. The book teaches opening from the third story and inviting the other to correct it, which converts the first five minutes from duelling indictments into joint fact finding. It also supplies the sentence architecture: describe the problem, state your purpose in having the conversation, and invite their perspective before delivering your own, an order that feels backwards and works forward.", "example": "A landlord tenant war reopens productively when the tenant begins not with the deposit accusation but with the third story: we signed a lease neither of us inspected well, the flat aged, we both feel owed. The landlord, unexecuted, produces receipts. The authors use such openings to show that neutrality at the start is not weakness; it is how information enters the room alive.", "action": "Write the third story of your current dispute in four neutral sentences. Open with it, and invite correction before conclusions."},
{"title": "Listening Is the Price of Being Heard", "chapter": "Listening and Expression", "summary": "The book's listening doctrine is transactional in the honest sense: people release their grip on a position roughly in proportion to feeling that its logic has been genuinely understood, not merely waited out. The skills are unglamorous, paraphrase their view until they confirm it, acknowledge the feelings inside it, ask the questions their story raises, and only then deliver yours, framed as your experience rather than the verdict. Couples and negotiators report the same astonishment: the paraphrase they resisted as surrender is what finally got their own case a hearing.", "example": "In a custody mediation transcript the authors annotate, each parent's position softens measurably only after the mediator's paraphrase of their fear is confirmed as accurate, word for word in places. Neither parent changed their request because they were persuaded; they changed it because they were, at last, sufficiently heard to become flexible. The mechanism generalises to every boardroom in the book.", "action": "In your next disagreement, paraphrase their position until they say that is right, before offering yours. Count the minutes it takes and what it buys."}
],
"this-is-going-to-hurt": [
{"title": "The Patient Stories Are the System's Report Card", "chapter": "Throughout the Diaries", "summary": "Between the farce and the catastrophe, the diaries keep filing clinical evidence: the woman whose cancer was missed through three referrals, the elderly patient warehoused between departments, the brilliant saves no system recorded. Adam Kay's comedy never quite distracts from the pattern that a service's real quality is decided by its tired moments and its handoffs. Health economists quote the book beside official reports because it supplies what the dashboards cannot: what the metrics felt like at the bedside at 4 a.m.", "example": "An entry describes discharging a patient safely despite three departments each believing another owned the case, the doctor functioning as the integration layer the software was supposed to be. Readers who work in any large institution recognise the role, and the risk it concentrates in conscientious individuals until they resign.", "action": "Find the human integration layer in your own system, the person compensating for its gaps, and write down what happens when they are on leave."},
{"title": "Why Doctors Leave: It Is Rarely the Medicine", "chapter": "The Resignation", "summary": "Adam Kay's exit interviews, given across years of the book's fame, keep returning to the same triad: hours that abolish a life, support absent at the moments of maximum trauma, and a culture that treats both as the profession's natural weather. His counterfactual is the book's policy legacy: he would still be delivering babies, a consultant by now, under merely humane conditions. Health systems that adopted Schwartz rounds, debriefs and sane rotas cite the memoir in their business cases, an unusual fate for a book with this many jokes about bodily fluids.", "example": "Former colleagues wrote to him after publication in numbers he describes as a second career's worth of correspondence, most saying some version of: your last chapter is mine, pending. The book became an unofficial exit survey of a workforce, which ministers discovered they could not un read in public argument.", "action": "Ask one health worker this month what would keep them in the job for ten more years. Believe the first answer; it is rarely pay alone."},
{"title": "The Public Read the Diary and Recognised the Ward", "chapter": "Reception as Evidence", "summary": "The book and its stage and television adaptations performed a rare civic function: they gave the abstract noun NHS a bloodstream, letting voters experience the system's abstractions as shifts, choices and losses. Adam Kay has used the platform for campaigns on doctor wellbeing and bereavement care, arguing that a public which has read the diary argues differently about funding, less in slogans, more in rotas. Whatever readers' politics, his reception chapters in interviews amount to a case study in how testimony moves policy where white papers stall.", "example": "The television adaptation's final episode, dramatising the catastrophe and its aftermath, drew public discussion onto doctor suicide and post incident support with a specificity that parliamentary debates had not achieved in a decade. The diary form, dates, exhaustion, jokes, had smuggled an inquiry into prime time.", "action": "When you next argue about a public service, replace one slogan with one shift: describe a real day inside it, yours or a borrowed diary's, in the argument."}
],
"year-of-magical-thinking": [
{"title": "You Sit Down to Dinner and Life as You Know It Ends", "chapter": "The Opening", "summary": "The book's famous first lines are also its thesis about knowledge: catastrophe has no prologue in the lives it takes, and the mind afterwards keeps returning to the ordinariness of the last normal hour as though ordinariness were a clue. Joan Didion reconstructs the evening forensically, the fire she had lit, the drink in his hand, the sentence he was finishing, because the bereaved are all detectives of this scene, searching it for the moment where intervention was possible. Her finding, that there is no such moment visible from inside a life, is the coldest comfort the book offers and the one readers quote back most gratefully.", "example": "She notes that he had predicted, reading his own unease, something like this, and that she had filed the remark as temperament. The bereaved reader recognises the filing system instantly, and the guilt attached to its ordinary operation. Joan Didion's precision declines to call it negligence, which is why widows and widowers trust her more than they trust consolation.", "action": "If you are litigating the last normal hour of anything, write the scene forensically once, then close the file for a defined season."},
{"title": "The Autopsy Report She Could Not Read", "chapter": "The Avoidances", "summary": "Among the book's most analysed avoidances: Joan Didion, who made a career of reading the documents others flinch from, could not for months read her husband's autopsy report, and avoided the hospital neighbourhood entirely. The detail matters because it universalises the magical thinking; if Joan Didion's instruments failed her here, no reader's failure is a character defect. She eventually reads everything, of course, and reports the reading with the same flat clarity, which is the book's implicit promise: the facts can be faced, on grief's schedule rather than competence's.", "example": "She describes realising she had been avoiding a street because it was the route to the hospital, only after catching herself mid detour, the kind of intelligence about oneself that arrives, in mourning, always slightly too late to be useful and exactly in time to be recorded. Grief therapists assign the passage to clients who believe their avoidances are unique pathologies.", "action": "Name one avoided document, street or song. You do not have to face it today. Naming is today's entire assignment."},
{"title": "Letting the Dead Be Dead", "chapter": "The Close of the Year", "summary": "Near the year's end Joan Didion confronts the movement she has resisted for three hundred pages: the photos must stop being portals, the shoes must be giveable, the dead must be allowed their new status or the living remain staff in a museum of one. She does not dramatise the surrender. It arrives as a series of clerical acts and one devastating recognition, that she had wanted not him back exactly, but the year before back, the world in which the phone call had not yet sorted everyone she loved into the living and the dead. The recognition, readers testify, is where their own mourning finally got its instructions.", "example": "She writes of realising that if she was to live herself she would have to let go, at least partially, and the grudging arithmetic of that sentence, at least partially, has been adopted wholesale by the bereaved as the most honest unit of progress available. No one in the book's readership reports letting go entirely. The partial is the permission.", "action": "Perform one clerical act of letting, partial and honest: one drawer, one subscription, one shoe box, at your own pace, this month."}
],
"midnight-library": [
{"title": "Small Things Carry the Big Importance", "chapter": "The Quiet Lives", "summary": "The novel's moral arithmetic inverts the highlight reel: a neighbour helped, a student encouraged, a brother answered kindly outweigh, in the lives where they compound, the shelves of medals and fame. Matt Haig plants the evidence early, a boy Nora Seed once taught swimming, a neighbour whose errands she ran, so the ending's revaluation is earned rather than asserted. Readers are being taught a metric: judge a life by the weight it carries in other lives at kitchen table distance, a scale on which most actual lives are far richer than their owners' ledgers record.", "example": "The swimmer she once coached reappears in her root life having built his own around the confidence those lessons installed, a return on kindness she had filed under nothing. The scene exists to indict every reader's filing system, because all of us are similarly owed and owing in currencies we have stopped counting.", "action": "Write down three returns on your past small kindnesses that you know of. Assume the ledger continues off the page. Let it affect today's accounting."},
{"title": "The Librarian Is the Voice That Stays", "chapter": "Mrs Elm", "summary": "Mrs Elm, the school librarian from Nora Seed's childhood, staffs the between life library, and the choice is the novel's quiet thesis about who saves us: not gurus but the adults who once made a safe, stocked room and treated our inner lives as real. She does not argue Nora Seed out of despair; she lends her books and lets experience teach, the librarian's method at cosmic scale. Teachers and librarians have adopted the novel in numbers that suggest the compliment landed exactly where it was addressed.", "example": "Their chess games and conversations reconstruct, shelf by shelf, a relationship in which Nora Seed was known at fifteen, the age before the contraction began. Matt Haig argues by casting: to be known early and accurately by one adult is protective equipment a person carries into every later midnight, which is why Mrs Elm gets the keys to the afterlife.", "action": "Thank one adult who stocked a safe room for your younger self, if they can still be thanked. If not, stock one for somebody else's fifteen year old."},
{"title": "It Is Not Too Late Is a Claim About Physics Here", "chapter": "The Ending's Argument", "summary": "Stories about regret usually run on redemption deadlines: the boat missed forever. Matt Haig's library abolishes the deadline instead, because parallel lives prove the opposite, that aliveness at any point reopens the shelves. Nora Seed's final education is temporal: the future of her root life is as unwritten as any volume she visited, and therefore as full of possible good. For readers whose despair speaks in perfect tenses, I have ruined, it is finished, the novel is engineered as a grammatical intervention disguised as a fantasy.", "example": "She returns to a life whose external facts have barely changed, cat, job, flat, brother, and the book asks us to watch the same inventory become a beginning rather than an ending purely through her recovered sense of its possibilities. The trick is the teaching: nothing in the root life needed to be replaced except the belief that it was over.", "action": "Take one perfect tense sentence about your life and rewrite it in the present continuous. Act on the new grammar once this week."}
]
}
